{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfzkUfAmVqLGqkQxvE8XV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tides are the rise and fall of sea level caused mainly by the Moon's gravity, with a smaller contribution from the Sun.\n\nThe Moon pulls on every part of Earth, but the pull is strongest on the side facing it and weakest on the far side. This difference in pull is called the tidal force. It stretches the ocean into a slight bulge toward the Moon. A second bulge forms on the opposite side of Earth. There, the Moon pulls the solid planet toward itself more than it pulls the water, so the water is left behind.\n\nEarth rotates beneath these two bulges once a day. Most coasts therefore see two high tides and two low tides in a bit more than 24 hours. The Moon also orbits Earth in the same direction Earth spins. Each day a given spot needs about 50 extra minutes to catch up with the Moon, so tides arrive roughly 50 minutes later each day.\n\nThe Sun's tidal effect is about half the Moon's. At new and full moon, the Sun, Moon, and Earth line up, and their effects add together. This produces spring tides, which have the highest highs and lowest lows. At the quarter moons, the Sun and Moon pull at right angles. This produces neap tides, which have a smaller range.\n\nLocal geography changes the picture a lot. Basin shape, coastline, and water depth can amplify, delay, or reshape the tides. In the Bay of Fundy, the range can exceed 15 meters. Some places have only one high tide a day, and some barely change at all."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":52,"cache_read_input_tokens":18363,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":52},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"pa